    {
      "id": "cpu.platform-clock-default",
      "name": "Remove forced platform clock overrides",
      "category": "System",
      "impact": "maintenance",
      "summary": "Lets Windows choose its own clock source instead of a forced platform timer.",
      "description": "Windows normally picks the cheapest reliable counter for QueryPerformanceCounter, on current PCs the processor's time stamp counter (TSC). Three boot options can override that: useplatformclock forces the motherboard's platform timer (HPET or ACPI PM timer), useplatformtick backs the system clock tick with a platform source, and tscsyncpolicy changes how the processors' TSCs are synchronised. Microsoft marks all three 'should only be used for debugging'. This deletes the three elements so Windows decides again. Reading a platform counter costs roughly 0.8 to 1.0 microsecond and a system call, a TSC read tens of cycles (Microsoft). No frame-rate or latency gain is claimed.",
      "risk": "systemConfiguration",
      "level": "moderate",
      "benefit": "documented",
      "valueBasis": "documented",
      "activation": "reboot",
      "limitations": "Does nothing on a PC where none of the three elements is set (Windows' default is no element). Needs a restart and administrator rights. It edits the boot configuration store; Microsoft notes BitLocker or Secure Boot may need to be suspended before BCDEdit changes on some PCs, but this only deletes values. Where Windows finds the TSC unusable it already selects a platform timer itself, so nothing changes there. For tscsyncpolicy Microsoft lists only Default, Legacy and Enhanced without describing the difference; deleting the element returns it to Default.",
      "source": "Microsoft Learn 'BCDEdit /set': useplatformclock 'Forces the use of the platform clock as the system's performance counter... should only be used for debugging'; useplatformtick 'Forces the clock to be backed by a platform source, no synthetic timers are allowed' (Windows 8+, debugging only); tscsyncpolicy [Default|Legacy|Enhanced] 'should only be used for debugging'; '/deletevalue' returns an option to its default. Microsoft Learn 'Acquiring high-resolution time stamps': Windows 8 and later use the TSC as the QPC basis, the platform counter costs about 0.8-1.0 us per call and needs a system call, the TSC 'tens or hundreds of machine cycles' with no kernel transition. https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/drivers/devtest/bcdedit--set and https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/sysinfo/acquiring-high-resolution-time-stamps",
      "sources": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/drivers/devtest/bcdedit--set",
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/sysinfo/acquiring-high-resolution-time-stamps"
      ],
      "system": [
        {
          "kind": "bcd",
          "name": "useplatformclock",
          "value": "absent"
        },
        {
          "kind": "bcd",
          "name": "useplatformtick",
          "value": "absent"
        },
        {
          "kind": "bcd",
          "name": "tscsyncpolicy",
          "value": "absent"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "cpu.win32-priority-separation-default",
      "name": "Restore the default foreground scheduling",
      "category": "System",
      "impact": "maintenance",
      "summary": "Puts Win32PrioritySeparation back to Windows' default of 2.",
      "description": "Win32PrioritySeparation is a 6-bit value that sets how long a thread runs each time it is scheduled (short or long), whether that length varies, and how much more processor time a foreground program gets than background ones. Microsoft documents the default as 2: on a Windows client, short, variable time slices where foreground threads get three times the processor time of background threads. The 'Programs' choice in Performance Options behaves the same; 'Background services' writes 0x18 (long, fixed slices, no foreground preference). This puts the default back if a tweak tool changed it. It undoes a change; no frame-rate or latency gain is claimed.",
      "risk": "systemConfiguration",
      "level": "safe",
      "benefit": "documented",
      "valueBasis": "documented",
      "activation": "reboot",
      "limitations": "Already 2 on a stock Windows (read as 0x2 on a Windows 11 Pro PC). The bit meanings come from an archived Windows 2000 reference page and an archived XP-era Microsoft blog; Microsoft publishes no Windows 10/11 page for this value, and the same value is interpreted differently on Windows Server, so this is for client Windows only. Values other than the default (0x26, 0x28, 0x2A, 0x16 and others circulated in tweak guides) are not documented as faster on Windows 10/11 and are not offered. A restart or sign-out is the safe way to be sure all processes pick it up.",
      "source": "Microsoft Learn (archived TechNet) 'Win32PrioritySeparation': HKLM\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\PriorityControl, REG_DWORD 0x0-0x3F, default 0x2; three 2-bit fields (interval length, variable vs fixed, foreground:background ratio); on a workstation the default 2 means shorter, variable intervals with foreground 3:1. Microsoft Learn archive blog 'Master Your Quantum': Performance Options writes 0x26 (Foreground) or 0x18 (Background); table of resulting quantums; changing the value needs no restart (XP). Read as 0x2 on a Windows 11 Pro PC (PC-000). https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/previous-versions/cc976120(v=technet.10)",
      "sources": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/previous-versions/cc976120(v=technet.10)",
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/archive/blogs/embedded/master-your-quantum"
      ],
      "registry": [
        {
          "hive": "HKLM",
          "path": "SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\PriorityControl",
          "name": "Win32PrioritySeparation",
          "type": "dword",
          "value": "2",
          "absentMatches": true
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "system.long-paths-on",
      "name": "Turn on Win32 long paths",
      "category": "System",
      "impact": "convenience",
      "summary": "Lets opted-in apps use file paths longer than 260 characters.",
      "description": "Windows file functions have a 260-character path limit (MAX_PATH). Since Windows 10 version 1607 that limit can be lifted, but only for apps that declare longPathAware in their manifest, and only when this machine-wide switch is on. The setting does nothing for apps that have not opted in.",
      "risk": "systemConfiguration",
      "level": "safe",
      "benefit": "documented",
      "valueBasis": "documented",
      "activation": "reboot",
      "minBuild": 14393,
      "limitations": "Only helps apps that declare long-path support (longPathAware in the manifest, or packaged Store apps that declare it); others keep failing on long paths. File Explorer and some shell dialogs may still refuse such paths. Microsoft says processes cache the value on first use, so a restart may be needed before every program sees it. Off by default (the value reads 0 on a Windows 11 Pro PC). No speed gain is claimed; this is a compatibility switch.",
      "source": "Microsoft Learn 'Maximum Path Length Limitation': the REG_DWORD HKLM\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\FileSystem\\LongPathsEnabled must exist and be 1, only apps whose manifest has longPathAware are affected, the value is cached per process, a reboot might be needed, and the same switch is the Group Policy 'Enable Win32 long paths'. Value 0 read on a Windows 11 Pro machine.",
      "sources": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/fileio/maximum-file-path-limitation"
      ],
      "registry": [
        {
          "hive": "HKLM",
          "path": "SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\FileSystem",
          "name": "LongPathsEnabled",
          "type": "dword",
          "value": "1"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "telemetry.ceip-policy-off",
      "name": "Turn off the Customer Experience Improvement Program (policy)",
      "category": "Privacy",
      "impact": "privacy",
      "summary": "Opts every user out of the Windows Customer Experience Improvement Program.",
      "description": "Sets the Group Policy 'Turn off Windows Customer Experience Improvement Program'. Microsoft documents it as opting all users out of the program, which collects hardware configuration and usage information to find trends. Windows' own CEIP scheduled tasks already say they do nothing without consent; this makes the opt-out a machine-wide policy instead of a per-PC consent. A privacy choice, not a speed-up.",
      "risk": "systemConfiguration",
      "level": "safe",
      "benefit": "documented",
      "valueBasis": "documented",
      "activation": "reboot",
      "requires": [
        "proEdition"
      ],
      "limitations": "No performance effect is documented or claimed. Windows default: policy not configured (the administrator can opt in through Problem Reports in Control Panel). A Group Policy value, so Microsoft lists it for Pro, Enterprise, Education and IoT Enterprise only; on Home the operation does not apply. Microsoft does not say when it takes effect, so Mesura asks for a restart. Turning CEIP off also switches Application Telemetry and the Inventory Collector off, according to Microsoft's own descriptions of those two policies. Microsoft's OS list starts at Windows 10 2004 with KB5005101 and Windows 11.",
      "source": "Microsoft Learn Policy CSP ADMX_ICM, policy CEIPEnable: 'Turn off Windows Customer Experience Improvement Program', key Software\\Policies\\Microsoft\\SQMClient\\Windows, value CEIPEnable, Device scope, Pro/Enterprise/Education (https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/client-management/mdm/policy-csp-admx-icm). Value semantics read from the ICM.admx shipped in C:\\Windows\\PolicyDefinitions on Windows 11 build 26200: enabledValue 0 (opted out), disabledValue 1 (opted in). Task descriptions read locally with Get-ScheduledTask (Consolidator, UsbCeip).",
      "sources": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/client-management/mdm/policy-csp-admx-icm"
      ],
      "registry": [
        {
          "hive": "HKLM",
          "path": "Software\\Policies\\Microsoft\\SQMClient\\Windows",
          "name": "CEIPEnable",
          "type": "dword",
          "value": "0"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "telemetry.application-telemetry-off",
      "name": "Turn off Application Telemetry (policy)",
      "category": "Privacy",
      "impact": "privacy",
      "summary": "Stops Windows tracking how apps use certain Windows system components.",
      "description": "Sets the Group Policy 'Turn off Application Telemetry'. Microsoft describes Application Telemetry as a mechanism that tracks anonymous usage of specific Windows system components by applications; turning it off stops that usage-data collection. It takes effect for newly launched applications at once. A privacy choice with no function behind it that the user sees.",
      "risk": "systemConfiguration",
      "level": "safe",
      "benefit": "documented",
      "valueBasis": "documented",
      "activation": "reboot",
      "requires": [
        "proEdition"
      ],
      "limitations": "Windows default: policy not configured, so the engine is on. Microsoft says running programs keep reporting until restarted, so Mesura asks for a restart. Microsoft also says Application Telemetry is already off when the Customer Experience Improvement Program is off, so on a PC that never opted into CEIP this changes nothing visible. Microsoft does not document which diagnostics rely on the data. No performance effect is documented. A Group Policy value for Pro, Enterprise, Education and IoT Enterprise; on Home the operation does not apply.",
      "source": "Microsoft Learn Policy CSP ADMX_AppCompat, AppCompatTurnOffApplicationImpactTelemetry: key Software\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\AppCompat, value AITEnable, description quoted above, 'reboot to ensure telemetry collection has stopped for all applications' (https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/client-management/mdm/policy-csp-admx-appcompat). Value direction read from AppCompat.admx in C:\\Windows\\PolicyDefinitions (Windows 11 build 26200): enabledValue 0 (telemetry off), disabledValue 1.",
      "sources": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/client-management/mdm/policy-csp-admx-appcompat"
      ],
      "registry": [
        {
          "hive": "HKLM",
          "path": "Software\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\AppCompat",
          "name": "AITEnable",
          "type": "dword",
          "value": "0"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "telemetry.limit-diagnostic-logs",
      "name": "Don't let Microsoft collect extra diagnostic logs (policy)",
      "category": "Privacy",
      "impact": "privacy",
      "summary": "Windows won't gather additional diagnostic logs when troubleshooting a problem.",
      "description": "Sets the Group Policy 'Limit Diagnostic Log Collection'. Microsoft says that by enabling it, additional diagnostic logs are not collected when more information is needed to troubleshoot a problem. Such logs are only ever sent when the device is set to send optional diagnostic data.",
      "risk": "systemConfiguration",
      "level": "safe",
      "benefit": "documented",
      "valueBasis": "documented",
      "activation": "reboot",
      "minBuild": 22000,
      "requires": [
        "proEdition"
      ],
      "limitations": "Only matters when optional diagnostic data is on: Microsoft says the logs are only sent in that case, and Mesura's 'Send required diagnostic data only' entry already keeps it off, so on that setup this value is a safeguard and changes nothing you can see. Windows default: not configured, which lets Microsoft occasionally collect logs when optional data is on. Microsoft documents the policy from Windows 11 21H2; Windows 10 is not listed on the current policy page. Pro, Enterprise, Education and IoT Enterprise only; on Home the operation does not apply.",
      "source": "Microsoft Learn Policy CSP System, LimitDiagnosticLogCollection: Device scope, Windows 11 21H2 [10.0.22000] and later, default 0, 1 = enabled, registry key Software\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\DataCollection (https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/client-management/mdm/policy-csp-system). Value name and enabledValue 1 read from DataCollection.admx and its English text in C:\\Windows\\PolicyDefinitions on Windows 11 build 26200 (valueName LimitDiagnosticLogCollection).",
      "sources": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/client-management/mdm/policy-csp-system"
      ],
      "registry": [
        {
          "hive": "HKLM",
          "path": "Software\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\DataCollection",
          "name": "LimitDiagnosticLogCollection",
          "type": "dword",
          "value": "1"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "telemetry.limit-dump-collection",
      "name": "Limit the crash dumps Microsoft can collect (policy)",
      "category": "Privacy",
      "impact": "privacy",
      "summary": "Error reports send only small dumps, never full or heap memory dumps.",
      "description": "Sets the Group Policy 'Limit Dump Collection'. Microsoft says that by enabling it, Windows Error Reporting is limited to sending kernel mini dumps and user-mode triage dumps. Without it, Microsoft may occasionally collect full or heap dumps, which can contain far more of a program's memory, when the user has opted in to optional diagnostic data.",
      "risk": "systemConfiguration",
      "level": "safe",
      "benefit": "documented",
      "valueBasis": "documented",
      "activation": "reboot",
      "minBuild": 22000,
      "requires": [
        "proEdition"
      ],
      "limitations": "Only matters when optional diagnostic data is on, because dumps are only sent in that case; with Mesura's 'Send required diagnostic data only' entry it is a safeguard rather than a visible change. It does not stop Windows Error Reporting itself (a separate entry does) and does not change dumps you keep locally. Smaller dumps can make a crash harder for Microsoft or an app vendor to diagnose. Windows default: not configured. Documented from Windows 11 21H2. Pro, Enterprise, Education and IoT Enterprise only; on Home the operation does not apply.",
      "source": "Microsoft Learn Policy CSP System, LimitDumpCollection: Device scope, Windows 11 21H2 [10.0.22000] and later, default 0, 1 = enabled, 'Windows Error Reporting is limited to sending kernel mini dumps and user mode triage dumps', registry key Software\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\DataCollection (https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/client-management/mdm/policy-csp-system). Value name and enabledValue 1 read from DataCollection.admx on Windows 11 build 26200 (valueName LimitDumpCollection).",
      "sources": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/client-management/mdm/policy-csp-system"
      ],
      "registry": [
        {
          "hive": "HKLM",
          "path": "Software\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\DataCollection",
          "name": "LimitDumpCollection",
          "type": "dword",
          "value": "1"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "privacy.no-inking-typing-data-upload",
      "name": "Don't send inking and typing data to improve recognition (policy)",
      "category": "Privacy",
      "impact": "privacy",
      "summary": "Windows stops sending your typing and handwriting samples to Microsoft.",
      "description": "Sets the Group Policy 'Improve inking and typing recognition' to disabled for every user. Microsoft describes it as the control over sending inking and typing data to Microsoft to improve the language recognition and suggestion capabilities of apps and services. Microsoft's privacy-connections page lists it, together with the user policy 'Turn off automatic learning', as the group-policy route to switching off Settings > Diagnostics & feedback > 'Improve inking and typing'; this entry sets only the first of the two. This is the upload; the separate 'Turn off typing and inking personalization' entry controls the local dictionary.",
      "risk": "systemConfiguration",
      "level": "safe",
      "benefit": "documented",
      "valueBasis": "documented",
      "activation": "signOut",
      "requires": [
        "proEdition"
      ],
      "limitations": "Microsoft lists no local function that stops working, but suggestion and recognition quality that Microsoft would have tuned from your data is not contributed to. Windows default: allowed (1). The Settings toggle becomes managed by your organisation. A Group Policy value, so Pro, Enterprise, Education and IoT Enterprise only; on Home the operation does not apply. Microsoft does not state when it takes effect, so Mesura asks for a sign-out.",
      "source": "Microsoft Learn Policy CSP TextInput, AllowLinguisticDataCollection: Device scope, default 1, 0 = not allowed, Group Policy 'Improve inking and typing recognition', key Software\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Policies\\TextInput, value AllowLinguisticDataCollection (https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/client-management/mdm/policy-csp-textinput). Microsoft's 'Manage connections from Windows operating system components to Microsoft services', section 18.21 Inking and typing: Settings 'Improve inking and typing' Off, Group Policy 'Improve inking and typing recognition' Disable (https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/privacy/manage-connections-from-windows-operating-system-components-to-microsoft-services). Value direction confirmed in TextInput.admx on Windows 11 build 26200 (enabledValue 1, disabledValue 0).",
      "sources": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/client-management/mdm/policy-csp-textinput",
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/privacy/manage-connections-from-windows-operating-system-components-to-microsoft-services"
      ],
      "registry": [
        {
          "hive": "HKLM",
          "path": "Software\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Policies\\TextInput",
          "name": "AllowLinguisticDataCollection",
          "type": "dword",
          "value": "0"
        }
      ]
