    {
      "id": "edge.no-startup-boost",
      "name": "Turn off Edge startup boost",
      "category": "Microsoft Edge",
      "impact": "performance",
      "summary": "Stops Edge from starting its processes at sign-in and relaunching them in the background.",
      "description": "Startup boost lets Microsoft Edge start processes when you sign in to Windows and restart them in the background after the last window is closed, so the browser opens faster. This sets the Edge policy StartupBoostEnabled to 0 (disabled), which turns startup boost off. Microsoft documents the policy, its registry value and its meaning; it does not publish how much memory or CPU the idle processes use.",
      "risk": "systemConfiguration",
      "level": "safe",
      "benefit": "unverified",
      "valueBasis": "documented",
      "activation": "immediate",
      "limitations": "Edge will show 'Managed by your organization' and the Startup boost switch in edge://settings/system is locked. The first Edge launch after sign-in can be slower. The effect on free memory or game performance is not measured by Microsoft and is not claimed here. Supported from Edge 88. Microsoft documents registry-based Edge policy for domain-joined or Intune-enrolled devices; on a standalone PC check edge://policy and confirm the policy is listed as applied, not blocked. Edge applies the change without a restart, but processes already running stay until Edge is closed. Not configured means Edge decides (Microsoft says it may be on or off initially).",
      "source": "Microsoft Learn, Edge policy StartupBoostEnabled: 'Allows Microsoft Edge processes to start at OS sign-in and restart in background after the last browser window is closed'; registry HKLM\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Edge, REG_DWORD, 0 disables it, Windows Edge 88 and later. Policy-on-registry scope from Microsoft Learn 'Configure Microsoft Edge for Windows with policy settings'.",
      "sources": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/deployedge/microsoft-edge-policies/startupboostenabled",
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/deployedge/configure-microsoft-edge"
      ],
      "registry": [
        {
          "hive": "HKLM",
          "path": "SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Edge",
          "name": "StartupBoostEnabled",
          "type": "dword",
          "value": "0"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "edge.diagnostic-data-required",
      "name": "Send only required diagnostic data from Edge",
      "category": "Microsoft Edge",
      "impact": "privacy",
      "summary": "Edge sends required diagnostic data only, no optional browsing and crash data.",
      "description": "This sets the Edge policy DiagnosticData to 1 (RequiredData). Required diagnostic data is what Microsoft says keeps Edge secure, up to date and performing as expected. Optional diagnostic data (value 2) includes how you use the browser, websites you visit and crash reports. Setting 1 turns optional collection off while keeping required data. From Edge 122 this policy works on Windows 10 and 11 separately from Windows' own diagnostic data setting.",
      "risk": "userPreference",
      "level": "safe",
      "benefit": "documented",
      "valueBasis": "documented",
      "activation": "immediate",
      "limitations": "Edge will show 'Managed by your organization'. Required data is still sent; value 0 (Off) exists but Microsoft marks it 'not recommended' and this entry does not use it. Needs an Edge restart (no dynamic refresh). On Edge 121 and earlier, Windows 10 ignores this policy and follows the Windows diagnostic data setting instead. Without the policy Edge follows the user's own preference. Crash reports no longer reach Microsoft's optional channel, which can make support harder.",
      "source": "Microsoft Learn, Edge policy DiagnosticData: Off (0), RequiredData (1) sends required data and 'turns off optional diagnostic data collection', OptionalData (2) includes 'data about browser usage, websites that are visited, crash reports'; REG_DWORD, Windows Edge 122+; 'Can be recommended: No'; restart required. Obsolete predecessors MetricsReportingEnabled and SendSiteInfoToImproveServices stop working after Edge 88.",
      "sources": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/deployedge/microsoft-edge-policies/diagnosticdata",
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/deployedge/microsoft-edge-policies/metricsreportingenabled",
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/deployedge/microsoft-edge-policies/sendsiteinfotoimproveservices"
      ],
      "registry": [
        {
          "hive": "HKLM",
          "path": "SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Edge",
          "name": "DiagnosticData",
          "type": "dword",
          "value": "1"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "cpu.dynamic-tick-default",
      "name": "Remove the forced constant timer tick",
      "category": "System",
      "impact": "maintenance",
      "summary": "Deletes the debugging option that keeps the system timer tick running when idle.",
      "description": "The BCD element disabledynamictick set to yes switches off Windows' dynamic timer tick, so the periodic system clock interrupt keeps firing even when nothing needs it. Microsoft documents the element as 'Enables and disables dynamic timer tick feature' and says it should only be used for debugging. This deletes it. Microsoft also documents that a faster system clock tends to raise power use; the 20 percent battery figure it gives is about the timer period programs request, not this option, and shows only the direction of the cost. No performance gain is claimed.",
      "risk": "systemConfiguration",
      "level": "moderate",
      "benefit": "unverified",
      "valueBasis": "documented",
      "activation": "reboot",
      "limitations": "Does nothing on a PC where the element is not set. Needs a restart. Microsoft's page does not state in words that dynamic tick is on by default; this is inferred from the element being a switch that turns the feature off, so treat the 'default' as the absence of the element. Some games or latency tools are reported by communities to behave differently with the tick forced on; that is anecdotal and not measured by Mesura. Edits the boot configuration store (BitLocker or Secure Boot may need suspending on some PCs per Microsoft).",
      "source": "Microsoft Learn 'BCDEdit /set': 'disabledynamictick [yes|no] Enables and disables dynamic timer tick feature. This option should only be used for debugging'; '/deletevalue' returns an option to its default. Microsoft Learn 'Results for the Idle Energy Efficiency Assessment': default system timer 15.6 ms, 1 ms drains the battery at least 20 percent faster; Microsoft Learn 'High-Resolution Timers': faster clock interrupts 'tend to increase power consumption'. https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/drivers/devtest/bcdedit--set",
      "sources": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/drivers/devtest/bcdedit--set",
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/test/assessments/results-for-the-idle-energy-efficiency-assessment",
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/drivers/kernel/high-resolution-timers"
      ],
      "system": [
        {
          "kind": "bcd",
          "name": "disabledynamictick",
          "value": "absent"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "cpu.core-parking-off",
      "name": "Keep all processor cores unparked (on AC)",
      "category": "Performance",
      "impact": "experimental",
      "summary": "Sets the core-parking minimum to 100% on AC so Windows stops parking cores.",
      "description": "Core parking lets Windows put some logical processors into a deep idle ('parked') state when load is low and wake them when load rises. The processor setting 'Processor performance core parking min cores' (powercfg alias CPMINCORES) is the minimum percentage of logical processors per NUMA node that may be unparked at any time. Microsoft documents that at 100 percent the core parking algorithm is disabled. This sets 100 on AC power in the active plan. The setting is hidden (visible only with powercfg /qh). No frame-rate or latency gain is claimed; the cost is the idle power of cores that stay awake.",
      "risk": "systemConfiguration",
      "level": "safe",
      "benefit": "unverified",
      "valueBasis": "documented",
      "activation": "immediate",
      "requires": [
        "noBattery"
      ],
      "limitations": "For desktops: skipped on a PC with a battery, and only the AC value of the active plan changes. Only the main core class is affected: on processors with performance and efficiency cores, the efficiency cores have a separate setting (CPMINCORES1) that this entry leaves alone, so they can still be parked. Windows' value depends on the plan and the processor: on the Windows 11 test PC the Balanced and High performance plans already read 100 on AC, so nothing changes there; on other PCs Balanced may park cores. Idle power use and heat rise slightly on PCs that did park cores. Microsoft says silicon vendors tune these settings and recommends consulting them first. The 'Ultimate Performance' or 'High performance' entries may already cover this.",
      "source": "Microsoft Learn 'CPMinCores': 'the minimum percentage of logical processors... that can be placed in the un-parked state at any given time... The Core Parking algorithm is disabled if the value of this setting is 100%'; range 0-100, powercfg alias CPMINCORES, hidden setting. Microsoft Learn 'Processor power management options' (note: silicon vendors tune PPM; contact them before modifying). GUIDs read from powercfg /qh on Windows 11 build 26200: subgroup 54533251-82be-4824-96c1-47b60b740d00 (SUB_PROCESSOR), setting 0cc5b647-c1df-4637-891a-dec35c318583 (Processor performance core parking min cores). https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/customize/power-settings/options-for-core-parking-cpmincores",
      "sources": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/customize/power-settings/options-for-core-parking-cpmincores",
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/customize/power-settings/configure-processor-power-management-options"
      ],
      "powerSettings": [
        {
          "subgroup": "54533251-82be-4824-96c1-47b60b740d00",
          "setting": "0cc5b647-c1df-4637-891a-dec35c318583",
          "name": "Processor performance core parking min cores",
          "ac": 100
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "power.nvme-stay-active-ac",
      "name": "Keep NVMe drives out of low-power idle (on AC)",
      "category": "Power",
      "impact": "experimental",
      "summary": "NVMe drives stay in their working state instead of idle power states on AC.",
      "description": "Sets the transition latency tolerance that Windows' in-box NVMe driver (StorNVMe) compares with a drive's entry plus exit latency to 0 ms on AC power, for both the primary and the secondary idle state. With no tolerance the driver finds no idle power state it may enter, so the drive stays in its working state between requests. Microsoft's built-in Performance scheme uses 0 ms on AC for both values; Balanced uses 15 ms (primary) and 100 ms (secondary) on the Windows 11 build 26200 test PC.",
      "risk": "systemConfiguration",
      "level": "safe",
      "benefit": "unverified",
      "valueBasis": "documented",
      "requires": [
        "noBattery"
      ],
      "activation": "immediate",
      "limitations": "Active plan only, AC only; battery behaviour isn't touched. Idle power and drive temperature rise a little. No measurement shows faster game loading or lower latency: the only documented change is that the drive no longer enters an idle state it must wake from. Applies to NVMe drives on Windows' in-box driver; Microsoft's page (written for Windows 10) doesn't cover vendor NVMe drivers and doesn't say whether a drive's own autonomous power-state transitions are affected. The Modern Standby row of Microsoft's table is separate and isn't changed. Defaults can differ by build and OEM; the recorded before-image is what gets restored.",
      "source": "Microsoft Learn 'NVMe' (power management for storage devices): GUIDs fc95af4d-... and dbc9e238-... are the primary/secondary latency tolerance in ms, 'the higher this value, the more likely that a deeper power state will be chosen', and the default table gives the Performance scheme 0 ms on AC for both. Values read from powercfg /qh and the DefaultPowerSchemeValues registry keys on a Windows 11 26200 PC (Balanced AC 15/100, High performance AC 0/0).",
      "sources": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/design/component-guidelines/power-management-for-storage-hardware-devices-nvme"
      ],
      "powerSettings": [
        {
          "subgroup": "0012ee47-9041-4b5d-9b77-535fba8b1442",
          "setting": "fc95af4d-40e7-4b6d-835a-56d131dbc80e",
          "name": "Primary NVMe power state transition latency tolerance",
          "ac": 0
        },
        {
          "subgroup": "0012ee47-9041-4b5d-9b77-535fba8b1442",
          "setting": "dbc9e238-6de9-49e3-92cd-8c2b4946b472",
          "name": "Secondary NVMe power state transition latency tolerance",
          "ac": 0
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "power.cpu-epp-performance-ac",
      "name": "Favour speed in the processor energy hint (on AC)",
      "category": "Power",
      "impact": "experimental",
      "summary": "Asks the CPU to favour speed over energy saving while on AC power.",
      "description": "Sets the processor energy performance preference (EPP) to 0 on AC power. Per Microsoft, 0 programs the CPU's energy performance preference register to 0, which favours performance; 100 programs 255, which favours energy saving. The setting exists for the main cores and, separately, for 'efficiency class 1' on CPUs with more than one core type; both are set so every core type gets the same hint. Windows' built-in High performance plan uses 0; Balanced uses 33 on AC on the Windows 11 build 26200 test PC.",
      "risk": "systemConfiguration",
      "level": "safe",
      "benefit": "unverified",
      "valueBasis": "documented",
      "requires": [
        "noBattery"
      ],
      "activation": "immediate",
      "limitations": "Microsoft documents the register being programmed on CPUs that implement CPPC version 2 with autonomous mode on. Windows ignores the autonomous-mode plan setting on platforms that support only one mode, so whether the value changes anything depends on the CPU, firmware and driver. The Power mode slider (Best performance/Better battery overlays) overrides the plan's processor settings; in the default mode the plan value applies. More heat, fan noise and idle power; battery behaviour isn't touched. No frame-rate or latency gain is documented or measured. Active plan only, AC only. The class 1 setting may not exist on every PC.",
      "source": "Microsoft Learn 'PerfEnergyPreference': alias PERFEPP/PERFEPP1, 0-100 %, 0 programs the EPP register to 0 'to favor performance', 100 to 255, only on CPPC v2 systems with autonomous mode enabled; 'PerfAutonomousMode'; 'Customize the Windows performance power slider' (overlays, and the advice to copy High performance settings into Balanced). GUIDs and per-plan defaults (Balanced 33, High performance 0, Power saver 60 on AC) read from powercfg /qh and DefaultPowerSchemeValues on a Windows 11 26200 PC.",
      "sources": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/customize/power-settings/options-for-perf-state-engine-perfenergypreference",
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/customize/power-settings/options-for-perf-state-engine-perfautonomousmode",
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/customize/desktop/customize-power-slider"
      ],
      "powerSettings": [
        {
          "subgroup": "54533251-82be-4824-96c1-47b60b740d00",
          "setting": "36687f9e-e3a5-4dbf-b1dc-15eb381c6863",
          "name": "Processor energy performance preference policy",
          "ac": 0
        },
        {
          "subgroup": "54533251-82be-4824-96c1-47b60b740d00",
          "setting": "36687f9e-e3a5-4dbf-b1dc-15eb381c6864",
          "name": "Processor energy performance preference policy for efficiency class 1",
          "ac": 0
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "power.cpu-ramp-up-fast-ac",
      "name": "Jump to top processor speed when load rises (on AC)",
      "category": "Power",
      "impact": "experimental",
      "summary": "Windows picks the highest processor performance state when load increases, on AC.",
      "description": "Sets the processor performance increase policy to 2 on AC power. Microsoft defines this value as selecting the highest speed/power performance state when the ideal state is higher than the current one, instead of the ideal state (0) or one step towards it (1). Windows' built-in High performance plan uses 2; Balanced uses 0 on the Windows 11 build 26200 test PC.",
      "risk": "systemConfiguration",
      "level": "safe",
      "benefit": "unverified",
      "valueBasis": "documented",
      "requires": [
        "noBattery"
      ],
      "activation": "immediate",
      "minBuild": 22000,
      "limitations": "Microsoft states it doesn't apply to CPUs running in autonomous performance mode, and its page lists Windows 11 only. On the test PC the plan setting for autonomous mode reads Enabled in all three built-in plans, so the value probably does nothing there; it matters only on CPUs whose performance state is chosen by Windows. Higher short-term power draw, heat and fan activity where it does act. No measured responsiveness or frame-time gain is available here. The efficiency-class-1 copy (PERFINCPOL1) is left alone: Windows keeps it at 3 in every built-in plan. Active plan only, AC only.",
      "source": "Microsoft Learn 'PerfIncreasePolicy': alias PERFINCPOL, values 0 ideal / 1 one step closer / 2 'highest speed/power processor performance state' / 3 ideal optimised for responsiveness, not applicable to autonomous performance states, Windows 11 x64. GUID and plan defaults (Balanced 0, High performance 2, Power saver 1) read from powercfg /qh and DefaultPowerSchemeValues on a Windows 11 26200 PC.",
      "sources": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/customize/power-settings/options-for-perf-state-engine-perfincreasepolicy"
      ],
      "powerSettings": [
        {
          "subgroup": "54533251-82be-4824-96c1-47b60b740d00",
          "setting": "465e1f50-b610-473a-ab58-00d1077dc418",
          "name": "Processor performance increase policy",
          "ac": 2
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "power.power-throttling-off",
      "name": "Turn off power throttling (policy)",
      "category": "Performance",
      "impact": "experimental",
      "summary": "Windows stops limiting CPU speed for background apps.",
      "description": "Turns on the 'Turn off Power Throttling' machine policy value. Power throttling puts apps that run in the background on the CPU's most efficient operating modes; Microsoft says it saves up to 11% CPU power and is always engaged unless the Power mode slider is set to Best performance. With the value set, Windows no longer throttles background apps.",
      "risk": "systemConfiguration",
      "level": "safe",
      "benefit": "unverified",
      "valueBasis": "documented",
      "activation": "reboot",
      "minBuild": 19041,
      "requires": [
        "noBattery",
        "proEdition"
      ],
      "limitations": "Meant for desktops: throttling exists to save battery, and this switch applies on battery too, so it isn't offered on PCs with a battery. Background work then runs at normal speed, which uses more power and can compete with a game for CPU time; Microsoft documents what the switch does but no measurement here shows it makes anything faster. Microsoft lists the policy for Pro, Enterprise, Education and IoT Enterprise (Windows 10 2004 with KB5005101 and later, Windows 11), so Home is excluded. On Windows 11 the Power mode slider on Best performance already opts every app out. Absence of the value means users control the setting (Windows default).",
      "source": "Microsoft Learn Policy CSP ADMX_Power 'PowerThrottlingTurnOff' (Turn off Power Throttling, Power.admx): key System\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\Power\\PowerThrottling, value PowerThrottlingOff, enabled = throttling off, not configured = users control; editions and builds as stated. Data value 1 (32-bit DWORD) and 'delete to re-enable' from two community guides (Winaero, Maketecheasier). Microsoft Learn 'Customize the Windows performance power slider' says throttling saves up to 11% CPU power and is bypassed on Best performance.",
      "sources": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/client-management/mdm/policy-csp-admx-power",
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/customize/desktop/customize-power-slider",
        "https://winaero.com/disable-power-throttling-windows-10-1709",
        "https://maketecheasier.com/disable-power-throttling-windows10"
      ],
      "registry": [
        {
          "hive": "HKLM",
          "path": "SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\Power\\PowerThrottling",
          "name": "PowerThrottlingOff",
          "type": "dword",
          "value": "1"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "dwm.window-animations-off",
      "name": "Turn off window animations (policy)",
      "category": "Appearance",
      "impact": "performance",
      "summary": "Stops the minimise, maximise and restore animations of windows.",
      "description": "Sets the Desktop Window Manager policy 'Do not allow window animations'. Windows then skips the animation played when a window is restored, minimised or maximised, so the window changes state at once. Microsoft documents that the policy takes effect after the user signs out and back in. Microsoft describes it only for window restore, minimise and maximise animations.",
      "risk": "systemConfiguration",
      "level": "safe",
      "benefit": "preference",
      "valueBasis": "documented",
      "activation": "signOut",
      "requires": [
        "proEdition"
      ],
      "limitations": "A look-and-feel change only; Microsoft documents no performance effect and Mesura does not claim one. Overlaps with the existing 'minimise animation' entry (that one is the per-user MinAnimate setting; this is the DWM policy). Microsoft lists the policy for Pro, Enterprise and Education, so Windows Home is treated as not applicable (proEdition). It takes effect at the next sign-in. How the animation switches in Settings behave while the policy is set was not tested. Windows default: not configured (animations on).",
      "source": "Microsoft Learn Policy CSP ADMX_DWM: policy 'Do not allow window animations' (DwmDisallowAnimations_1, User scope; key SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\DWM, value DisallowAnimations; 'If you enable this policy setting, window animations are turned off'; 'Changing this policy setting requires a logoff'; Windows 11 21H2 and later, Pro/Enterprise/Education). The DWORD type and enabled value 1 were read from DWM.admx in C:\\Windows\\PolicyDefinitions on Windows 11 build 26200 (enabledValue decimal 1, disabledValue decimal 0).",
      "sources": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/client-management/mdm/policy-csp-admx-dwm"
      ],
      "registry": [
        {
          "hive": "HKCU",
          "path": "SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\DWM",
          "name": "DisallowAnimations",
          "type": "dword",
          "value": "1"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "gaming.game-recording-policy-off",
      "name": "Disallow Windows Game Recording and Broadcasting (policy)",
      "category": "Graphics",
      "impact": "gaming",
      "summary": "Machine-wide policy that blocks Game Bar recording and broadcasting.",
      "description": "Sets the documented AllowGameDVR policy to 0, which tells Windows not to allow Windows Game Recording and Broadcasting for any user. It is the machine-wide, documented form of turning off Game Bar captures; the per-user catalog entries use community-documented values instead. Microsoft states that if the setting is enabled or not configured, recording and broadcasting are allowed (the default).",
      "risk": "systemConfiguration",
      "level": "safe",
      "benefit": "unverified",
      "valueBasis": "documented",
      "activation": "signOut",
      "requires": [
        "proEdition"
      ],
      "limitations": "Game Bar recording and broadcasting stop being allowed for every user until the policy is removed. Microsoft's performance effect is not documented: Windows ships with background recording off, so the saving is nil unless recording had been turned on. Microsoft's page notes the policy is 'only enforced in Windows 10 for desktop'; enforcement on Windows 11 is not spelled out, and a registry read-back confirms the value but not the behaviour. Whether Game Bar screenshots are affected is not documented. Ignored by Windows Home (proEdition). Windows default: not configured (allowed).",
      "source": "Microsoft Learn Policy CSP ApplicationManagement/AllowGameDVR: 'This setting enables or disables the Windows Game Recording and Broadcasting features. If you disable this setting, Windows Game Recording won't be allowed. If the setting is enabled or not configured, then Recording and Broadcasting (streaming) will be allowed'; 0 = Not allowed, 1 = Allowed (default); Device scope; Pro/Enterprise/Education; Windows 10 1507 and later; Group Policy 'Enables or disables Windows Game Recording and Broadcasting', key Software\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\GameDVR, value AllowGameDVR. GameDVR.admx on Windows 11 build 26200 confirms the same key/value with enabledValue decimal 1 and disabledValue decimal 0.",
      "sources": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/client-management/mdm/policy-csp-applicationmanagement"
      ],
      "registry": [
        {
          "hive": "HKLM",
          "path": "SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\GameDVR",
          "name": "AllowGameDVR",
          "type": "dword",
          "value": "0"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "visual.transparency-off",
      "name": "Turn off transparency effects",
      "category": "Appearance",
      "impact": "performance",
      "summary": "Makes the taskbar, Start and other surfaces opaque instead of see-through.",
      "description": "Turns off Windows' 'Transparency effects' (the Settings > Personalization > Colors switch, stored per user as EnableTransparency: 1 = on, 0 = off). Surfaces such as the taskbar, Start menu and system flyouts are drawn solid, so the compositor no longer blends and blurs what is behind them. Microsoft's performance guide says Windows 11 visual effects use system resources and that reducing them can improve responsiveness; it does not name transparency specifically.",
      "risk": "userPreference",
      "level": "safe",
      "benefit": "unverified",
      "valueBasis": "community",
      "activation": "explorerRestart",
      "limitations": "No measured gain is documented; any saving is small and mostly relevant on weak integrated graphics. Looks flatter. Per-user. Windows default: on (value 1 is present on a stock profile). One source says to restart the PC or sign out for it to apply everywhere. Do not combine with a claim of FPS improvement in games: games drawn in exclusive or flip-model presentation do not use this effect.",
      "source": "Two independent how-tos agree on path, type and meaning: Winaero ('HKCU\\Software\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Themes\\Personalize', 32-bit DWORD EnableTransparency, 1 = enable, 0 = disable) and NinjaOne (same path, REG_DWORD, 1 = enables, 0 = disables transparency effects for the current user; its PowerShell example has the values reversed, which contradicts its own text). Value read as 1 on Windows 11 build 26200 (default on). Microsoft support 'Tips to improve PC performance in Windows': visual effects can use system resources, 'Adjust for best performance' can improve responsiveness (transparency not named).",
      "sources": [
        "https://winaero.com/how-to-enable-or-disable-transparency-in-windows-11/",
        "https://www.ninjaone.com/blog/enable-or-disable-transparency-effects-in-windows-11/",
        "https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/tips-to-improve-pc-performance-in-windows-b3b3ef5b-5953-fb6a-2528-4bbed82fba96"
      ],
      "registry": [
        {
          "hive": "HKCU",
          "path": "Software\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Themes\\Personalize",
          "name": "EnableTransparency",
          "type": "dword",
          "value": "0"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "gaming.controller-game-bar-off",
      "name": "Don't open Game Bar from the controller's Xbox button",
      "category": "Graphics",
      "impact": "gaming",
      "summary": "Stops the Xbox/Guide button on a controller from opening Game Bar.",
      "description": "Turns off Settings > Gaming > Game Bar > 'Allow your controller to open Game Bar'. Microsoft's Windows 11 settings reference names the value (UseNexusForGameBarEnabled, REG_DWORD, 0 or 1) as the key behind that toggle; community guides and a Microsoft Q&A answer agree that 0 is off. It prevents Game Bar from popping up over a game when the controller button is pressed by accident.",
      "risk": "userPreference",
      "level": "safe",
      "benefit": "preference",
      "valueBasis": "community",
      "activation": "reboot",
      "limitations": "Only the controller shortcut is removed; Win+G and the Game Bar app itself are untouched. Microsoft documents the value only as the key behind this toggle and does not say which of 0 or 1 is on. The 0 = off direction comes from a Microsoft Q&A answer and community guides. The default is disputed (a Windows 10 tutorial says on, a Windows 11 tutorial says off), and the value is absent on a fresh profile such as this research PC. On a Windows 11 PC where it is already off, applying it changes nothing. A Q&A answer says to restart Windows afterwards. Undo restores the recorded before-image (a deleted value if it was absent). Brings no documented performance change; it is an interface preference.",
      "source": "Microsoft Learn, Reference for Windows 11 settings > Gaming: HKCU\\Software\\Microsoft\\GameBar, UseNexusForGameBarEnabled, REG_DWORD 0 or 1, 'Key associated with toggle Gaming -> Game Bar -> Allow your controller to open Game Bar' (https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/apps/develop/settings/settings-windows-11). Microsoft Q&A answer (community) sets it to 0 to stop the controller button opening Game Bar and says to restart (https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/answers/questions/5892017/need-to-prevent-gamebar-from-opening-ever-under-an). 0 = off is community-established (Ten Forums/Eleven Forum tutorials seen only as search snippets; pages returned 403). Default is disputed between those tutorials.",
      "sources": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/apps/develop/settings/settings-windows-11",
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/answers/questions/5892017/need-to-prevent-gamebar-from-opening-ever-under-an"
      ],
      "registry": [
        {
          "hive": "HKCU",
          "path": "Software\\Microsoft\\GameBar",
          "name": "UseNexusForGameBarEnabled",
          "type": "dword",
          "value": "0"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "network.netbios-over-tcpip-off",
      "name": "Turn off NetBIOS over TCP/IP on every adapter",
      "category": "Network",
      "impact": "privacy",
      "summary": "Stops legacy NetBIOS name broadcasts and the NBT ports; names resolve via DNS",
      "description": "NetBIOS over TCP/IP (NetBT) is the legacy name service Windows can fall back to when DNS cannot resolve a short host name. Each network interface has a NetbiosOptions value: 0 uses the DHCP server's setting, 1 enables NetBIOS, 2 disables it. This sets NetbiosOptions = 2 on every interface under NetBT\\Parameters\\Interfaces (the same switch as 'Disable NetBIOS over TCP/IP' in the adapter's WINS tab).",
      "risk": "systemConfiguration",
      "level": "moderate",
      "benefit": "preference",
      "valueBasis": "documented",
      "activation": "reboot",
      "limitations": "Anything that still depends on NetBIOS name lookup stops working on that interface: finding older NAS boxes, printers or PCs by short name without DNS or LLMNR, SMB1-era network browsing and some very old software. Modern file sharing (SMB over port 445), DNS names and IP addresses are unaffected. Security vendors commonly recommend turning NBT-NS off because its broadcast answers can be spoofed on a shared network; that is their guidance, not a Microsoft statement. Normal default: 0 (use the DHCP setting). A change applies after 'ipconfig /renew' on DHCP interfaces, otherwise after a restart. Interfaces created later (new adapters, VPNs) are not covered until the setting is applied again.",
      "source": "Microsoft Learn (unattend reference, NetBT NetbiosOptions): 0 = use the DHCP setting if available, 1 = NetBIOS enabled (default when DHCP is not available), 2 = NetBIOS disabled. Microsoft Learn archived 'Appendix B: NetBIOS over TCP/IP Configuration Parameters': NetbiosOptions is a REG_DWORD per interface under NetBT\\Parameters\\Interfaces, changes apply after ipconfig /renew or reboot. Key name form Tcpip_{GUID} confirmed by a read-only look at a Windows 11 PC and by the NetworkingDsc NetBios resource.",
      "sources": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/customize/desktop/unattend/microsoft-windows-netbt-interfaces-interface-netbiosoptions",
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/previous-versions/windows/it-pro/windows-server-2003/cc775874(v=ws.10)",
        "https://www.powershellgallery.com/packages/NetworkingDsc/7.2.0.0/Content/DSCResources%5CMSFT_NetBios%5CMSFT_NetBios.psm1"
      ],
      "registryEach": [
        {
          "hive": "HKLM",
          "pathPattern": "SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Services\\NetBT\\Parameters\\Interfaces\\*",
          "name": "NetbiosOptions",
          "type": "dword",
          "value": "2"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "network.wifi-power-saving-max-performance",
      "name": "Wi-Fi adapter power saving: Maximum Performance (on AC)",
      "category": "Power",
      "impact": "experimental",
      "summary": "Sets the wireless adapter's power-saving mode to Maximum Performance when plugged in",
      "description": "The Wireless Adapter Settings > Power Saving Mode setting of a power plan tells the Wi-Fi driver how aggressively to save power: 0 Maximum Performance, 1 Low Power Saving, 2 Medium Power Saving, 3 Maximum Power Saving. This sets it to 0 for the AC (plugged in) side of the active plan.",
      "risk": "userPreference",
      "level": "safe",
      "benefit": "unverified",
      "valueBasis": "community",
      "activation": "immediate",
      "limitations": "Only the AC value is changed; on battery the laptop behaves as before. Intel says Maximum Performance favours performance over power saving, that on battery it drains the charge faster, and that it strongly recommends keeping the default wireless power settings (Low and Medium Power Saving behave the same on its adapters). On many PCs this changes nothing: the Balanced plan already has AC = 0 (Maximum Performance) on the Windows 11 test PC; it matters where an OEM or custom plan sets a higher AC value. The effect on latency or throughput depends on the Wi-Fi driver and is not measured. The setting's GUID and index meanings come from powercfg output, Intel's article and third-party guides; Microsoft documents no page for this GUID. Windows or the OEM may hide the entry in the power options, but the value can still be read and written. Active plan only.",
      "source": "GUIDs and value names read from this Windows 11 PC with powercfg /qh: subgroup 19cbb8fa-5279-450e-9fac-8a3d5fedd0c1 (Wireless Adapter Settings), setting 12bbebe6-58d6-4636-95bb-3217ef867c1a (Power Saving Mode), indexes 0 Maximum Performance, 1 Low Power Saving, 2 Medium Power Saving, 3 Maximum Power Saving; Balanced plan AC = 0, DC = 2. Intel support article on wireless power settings describes the modes. The learn.microsoft.com power-setting page for this GUID returned 404 when fetched, so the GUIDs rest on the OS's own powercfg output plus Intel's description.",
      "sources": [
        "https://www.intel.com/content/www/us/en/support/articles/000005879/wireless.html"
      ],
      "powerSettings": [
        {
          "subgroup": "19cbb8fa-5279-450e-9fac-8a3d5fedd0c1",
          "setting": "12bbebe6-58d6-4636-95bb-3217ef867c1a",
          "name": "Wireless adapter power saving mode",
          "ac": 0
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "network.llmnr-off",
      "name": "Turn off LLMNR",
      "category": "Network",
      "impact": "privacy",
      "summary": "Stops Windows asking the local subnet to resolve names that DNS could not.",
      "description": "LLMNR (link-local multicast name resolution) is a secondary name resolution protocol. When DNS cannot resolve a short name, Windows can send a multicast query to other PCs on the same subnet and accept any answer. This sets the DNS Client policy 'Turn off multicast name resolution', which Microsoft documents as disabling LLMNR on all available network adapters. DNS is tried first, so ordinary DNS names are not affected.",
      "risk": "systemConfiguration",
      "level": "moderate",
      "benefit": "documented",
      "valueBasis": "documented",
      "activation": "reboot",
      "requires": [
        "proEdition"
      ],
      "limitations": "A hardening choice, not a speed-up; no performance claim is made. Short names that only LLMNR resolves stop working: another PC or NAS on a home network that your router's DNS does not know, reached by its bare name. Windows can still fall back to NetBIOS or mDNS where those are on. Not configured means LLMNR stays on, so Mesura does not treat an absent value as already matching. Machine policy: ignored by Windows Home. Takes effect after a restart.",
      "source": "Microsoft Learn Policy CSP ADMX_DnsClient, Turn_Off_Multicast: 'Turn off multicast name resolution', registry key Software\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\DNSClient, value EnableMulticast; enabling the policy disables LLMNR on all adapters, disabling or not configuring leaves it enabled; Pro/Enterprise/Education, Windows 10 2004+ and Windows 11. Confirmed against the shipped dnsclient.admx on a Windows 11 26200 PC (enabledValue 0). DNS-then-LLMNR-then-NetBT order from the DNS_SmartMultiHomedNameResolution description on the same page.",
      "sources": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/client-management/mdm/policy-csp-admx-dnsclient"
      ],
      "registry": [
        {
          "hive": "HKLM",
          "path": "SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\DNSClient",
          "name": "EnableMulticast",
          "type": "dword",
          "value": "0"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "network.ipv6-6to4-isatap-off",
      "name": "Turn off 6to4 and ISATAP tunnelling",
      "category": "Network",
      "impact": "privacy",
      "summary": "Removes the 6to4 and ISATAP IPv6-over-IPv4 tunnel interfaces; native IPv6 stays.",
      "description": "6to4 and ISATAP are transition tricks that carry IPv6 inside IPv4. 6to4 is enabled when an interface has a public IPv4 address and then registers its 6to4 address in DNS; ISATAP is off by default (Microsoft's IPv6 guidance). This sets the two 'IPv6 Transition Technologies' policies (6to4 State, ISATAP State) to Disabled. Native IPv6 and the Teredo setting are left alone. Microsoft lists this policy as one of two documented ways to switch tunnel interfaces off and advises against disabling IPv6 itself; RFC 7526 requires unicast 6to4 to be disabled by default in hosts.",
      "risk": "systemConfiguration",
      "level": "safe",
      "benefit": "documented",
      "valueBasis": "documented",
      "activation": "reboot",
      "requires": [
        "proEdition"
      ],
      "limitations": "On most home PCs behind a router (private IPv4 address, no ISATAP router) there is no 6to4 or ISATAP interface to begin with, so nothing visible changes; the value is a guarantee, not a speed-up, and no performance gain is claimed. Microsoft's pages disagree on 6to4's default (KB 929852 says on with a public IPv4 address; the Policy CSP page says the policy default is off), which is why the value is set explicitly. Corporate setups that use ISATAP (DirectAccess, older intranets) need it; do not use there. Machine policy: ignored by Windows Home. Needs a restart.",
      "source": "Microsoft Learn Policy CSP ADMX_tcpip: 'Set 6to4 State' and 'Set ISATAP State', key Software\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\TCPIP\\v6Transition; Disabled = 'No ... interfaces are present' / 6to4 turned off. Value names 6to4_State and ISATAP_State with string values Default/Enabled/Disabled confirmed in the shipped tcpip.admx (Windows 11 26200). Microsoft Learn KB 929852 'Configure IPv6 for advanced users': Group Policy 6to4/ISATAP/Teredo State = Disabled is the documented way to switch off tunnel interfaces; 'ISATAP and Teredo are disabled by default'; we don't recommend disabling IPv6. RFC 7526 section 5: 6to4 must be off by default on hosts.",
      "sources": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/client-management/mdm/policy-csp-admx-tcpip",
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/troubleshoot/windows-server/networking/configure-ipv6-in-windows",
        "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7526.html"
      ],
      "registry": [
        {
          "hive": "HKLM",
          "path": "SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\TCPIP\\v6Transition",
          "name": "6to4_State",
          "type": "string",
          "value": "Disabled"
        },
        {
          "hive": "HKLM",
          "path": "SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\TCPIP\\v6Transition",
          "name": "ISATAP_State",
          "type": "string",
          "value": "Disabled"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "network.smb1-off",
      "name": "Remove SMB 1.0 support",
      "category": "Network",
      "impact": "components",
      "summary": "Turns off the obsolete SMB 1.0 file-sharing feature (mostly already absent).",
      "description": "SMB 1.0 is the old file-sharing protocol that Microsoft deprecated in 2014 and strongly advises against. It is not installed on a clean Windows 11 install, but it can still be present on upgraded systems and on Windows 10 Home and Pro installs. This turns off the SMB1Protocol optional feature.",
      "risk": "systemConfiguration",
      "level": "moderate",
      "benefit": "documented",
      "valueBasis": "documented",
      "activation": "reboot",
      "limitations": "Devices and software that only speak SMB 1.0 (old NAS boxes, old scan-to-folder printers, ancient Linux or Samba servers) can no longer connect; Windows shows 'This share requires the obsolete SMB1 protocol'. On Windows 11 the feature is not installed by default, and on Windows 10 an unused SMB1 client is removed automatically after 15 days, so this often changes nothing; a PC that still has it enabled has probably used it. Removing it also removes the legacy Computer Browser method of filling Explorer's Network list. Needs a restart. No speed gain is claimed.",
      "source": "Microsoft Learn 'Detect, enable, and disable SMBv1, SMBv2, and SMBv3': 'SMBv1 has significant security vulnerabilities, and we strongly encourage you not to use it'; Disable-WindowsOptionalFeature -FeatureName SMB1Protocol; restart; not installed by default in Windows 11. Microsoft Learn 'SMBv1 Not Installed by Default': deprecated 2014; Windows 10 Home/Pro clean installs: client present by default until unused 15 days (Pro no longer from 1809); error text when a device needs SMB1; Computer Browser removed with SMB1. I could not run Get-WindowsOptionalFeature (needs elevation), so the feature name is from Microsoft's page, not from this PC.",
      "sources": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/storage/file-server/troubleshoot/detect-enable-and-disable-smbv1-v2-v3",
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/storage/file-server/troubleshoot/smbv1-not-installed-by-default-in-windows"
      ],
      "features": [
        {
          "name": "SMB1Protocol",
          "enabled": false
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "network.remote-assistance-off",
      "name": "Turn off Solicited Remote Assistance",
      "category": "Network",
      "impact": "privacy",
      "summary": "Blocks Remote Assistance invitations sent by email, file or instant message.",
      "description": "Windows Remote Assistance lets someone you invite (by file, email or instant message) view or take control of your session. This sets the policy 'Configure Solicited Remote Assistance' to Disabled; Microsoft's policy text says users then cannot use email or file transfer to ask someone for help, nor instant-messaging programs to allow connections. The DISA Windows 11 STIG requires the same value. The policy text does not mention Remote Desktop, Quick Assist or the separate 'Offer Remote Assistance' policy; they are not part of this entry.",
      "risk": "systemConfiguration",
      "level": "safe",
      "benefit": "documented",
      "valueBasis": "documented",
      "activation": "reboot",
      "requires": [
        "proEdition"
      ],
      "limitations": "Remote Assistance (msra.exe) invitations stop working, including 'Ask for help' from this PC. Quick Assist is a separate app that the policy text does not mention; Mesura makes no claim about it. The Windows default here is on (HKLM\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\Remote Assistance fAllowToGetHelp = 1), but the feature only opens a listener while an invitation is active. Machine policy: ignored by Windows Home. Needs a restart to be sure. A hardening choice, not a speed-up.",
      "source": "Shipped RemoteAssistance.admx / .adml on Windows 11 26200: policy RA_Solicit, key Software\\policies\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\Terminal Services, value fAllowToGetHelp, enabled=1, disabled=0; text 'If you disable this policy setting, users on this computer cannot use email or file transfer to ask someone for help'. DISA Windows 11 STIG V-253382 'Solicited Remote Assistance must not be allowed': HKLM\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\Terminal Services\\fAllowToGetHelp REG_DWORD 0.",
      "sources": [
        "https://www.stigviewer.com/stigs/microsoft_windows_11_v2r8/2026-05-19/finding/V-253382"
      ],
      "registry": [
        {
          "hive": "HKLM",
          "path": "SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\Terminal Services",
          "name": "fAllowToGetHelp",
          "type": "dword",
          "value": "0"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "privacy.no-settings-sync",
      "name": "Turn off settings sync (policy)",
      "category": "Privacy",
      "impact": "privacy",
      "summary": "Windows stops syncing your settings and passwords through your Microsoft account.",
      "description": "Turns off Windows 'sync your settings' for every user of this PC: none of the sync groups (themes, passwords, language, accessibility and others) is synced to or from this PC, and the switch is disabled so users cannot turn it back on. A privacy choice, not a speed-up.",
      "risk": "systemConfiguration",
      "level": "safe",
      "benefit": "documented",
      "valueBasis": "documented",
      "activation": "signOut",
      "requires": [
        "proEdition"
      ],
      "limitations": "Themes, saved Wi-Fi/password data, language and other preferences no longer follow your Microsoft account to your other PCs, and a new PC will not receive them from this one. Settings shows the option as managed by your organisation. Group Policy values: Microsoft lists them for Pro, Enterprise and Education, so on Home they may be ignored. Sync built into Edge or other apps is a separate setting and is not changed. Microsoft's pages call the UI 'Sync your settings' and, on newer Windows 11 builds, 'Windows backup'; which Settings page is switched off on every build was not checked. Windows default: sync on and user-controlled (values absent).",
      "source": "Microsoft Learn 'Manage connections from Windows operating system components to Microsoft services' item 21: HKLM\\Software\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\SettingSync DisableSettingSync = 2 and DisableSettingSyncUserOverride = 1 (Group Policy 'Do not sync', 'Allow users to turn syncing on' left unchecked). Policy CSP ADMX_SettingSync 'DisableSettingSync': enabling turns 'sync your settings' off so none of the groups is synced; Pro/Enterprise/Education, Windows 10 2004+ and Windows 11. The local SettingSync.admx (Windows 11 build 26200) confirms enabledValue 2 and that the unchecked 'Allow users...' box writes DisableSettingSyncUserOverride = 1.",
      "sources": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/privacy/manage-connections-from-windows-operating-system-components-to-microsoft-services",
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/client-management/mdm/policy-csp-admx-settingsync",
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/client-management/mdm/policy-csp-experience"
      ],
      "registry": [
        {
          "hive": "HKLM",
          "path": "SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\SettingSync",
          "name": "DisableSettingSync",
          "type": "dword",
          "value": "2"
        },
        {
          "hive": "HKLM",
          "path": "SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\SettingSync",
          "name": "DisableSettingSyncUserOverride",
          "type": "dword",
          "value": "1"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "privacy.no-cross-device-clipboard",
      "name": "Turn off clipboard sync across devices (policy)",
      "category": "Privacy",
      "impact": "privacy",
      "summary": "Copied text is never sent to the cloud to appear on your other PCs.",
      "description": "Stops clipboard contents from being synchronized across devices signed in with the same Microsoft or work account. When allowed, a copied item is uploaded to the cloud so other devices can paste it; with this policy it stays on this PC. Local clipboard history (Win+V) is a separate setting and is not changed.",
      "risk": "systemConfiguration",
      "level": "safe",
      "benefit": "documented",
      "valueBasis": "documented",
      "activation": "immediate",
      "minBuild": 17763,
      "requires": [
        "proEdition"
      ],
      "limitations": "If you use 'Sync across your devices' to paste between PCs, that stops working. Microsoft does not say how Settings shows the toggle afterwards (not verified). It is a Group Policy value: Microsoft lists it for Pro, Enterprise and Education. Per Microsoft the policy takes effect immediately. Windows default: allowed (value absent); on a normal install the user must still turn the sync on themselves, so for many people this entry only blocks a future opt-in (the user-level default was seen only in community guides).",
      "source": "Microsoft Learn Policy CSP Privacy 'AllowCrossDeviceClipboard': 0 = not allowed, 1 (default) = allowed; 'When disabled, Clipboard contents can't be shared to other devices'; takes effect immediately; Windows 10 1809+; Pro/Enterprise/Education; Group Policy 'Allow Clipboard synchronization across devices', key Software\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\System, value AllowCrossDeviceClipboard (OSPolicy.admx). Learn 'Manage connections...' item 30 'Cloud Clipboard' (most restricted value 0). Local OSPolicy.admx (build 26200) confirms enabledValue 1 / disabledValue 0. A Tenable CIS audit page recommends Disabled for the same policy.",
      "sources": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/client-management/mdm/policy-csp-privacy",
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/privacy/manage-connections-from-windows-operating-system-components-to-microsoft-services",
        "https://tenable.com/audits/items/CIS_MS_Windows_10_Enterprise_Level_2_v1.10.1.audit:f4f5e4a47a72ad370ef0f1a5898b38bb"
      ],
      "registry": [
        {
          "hive": "HKLM",
          "path": "SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\System",
          "name": "AllowCrossDeviceClipboard",
          "type": "dword",
          "value": "0"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
