    {
      "id": "edge.no-background-mode",
      "name": "Stop Edge running after its last window closes",
      "category": "Microsoft Edge",
      "impact": "performance",
      "summary": "Edge exits fully when you close the last window instead of staying in the tray.",
      "description": "With background mode on, Edge starts at sign-in and keeps running after the last window is closed, so background apps and session cookies stay active, with an icon in the system tray. This sets the Edge policy BackgroundModeEnabled to 0, which turns background mode off, so closing the last window ends the Edge process.",
      "risk": "systemConfiguration",
      "level": "safe",
      "benefit": "unverified",
      "valueBasis": "documented",
      "activation": "immediate",
      "limitations": "Edge will show 'Managed by your organization' and the background-apps switch in edge://settings/system is locked. Sites and web apps that rely on Edge staying alive (push notifications, installed web apps, background sync) stop receiving anything while Edge is closed. Supported from Edge 77. Microsoft documents registry-based Edge policy for domain-joined or Intune-enrolled devices; on a standalone PC confirm in edge://policy that it is applied. Microsoft's page says that when not configured the mode is initially off and the user can change it, so on many PCs this only locks the existing state. Microsoft does not publish the resource cost of the background process.",
      "source": "Microsoft Learn, Edge policy BackgroundModeEnabled: 'Allows Microsoft Edge processes to start at OS sign-in and keep running after the last browser window is closed... An open background process displays an icon in the system tray'; REG_DWORD under HKLM\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Edge; Windows Edge 77 and later.",
      "sources": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/deployedge/microsoft-edge-policies/backgroundmodeenabled",
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/deployedge/configure-microsoft-edge"
      ],
      "registry": [
        {
          "hive": "HKLM",
          "path": "SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Edge",
          "name": "BackgroundModeEnabled",
          "type": "dword",
          "value": "0"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "requires": [ "hybridCpu" ],
      "id": "cpu.hybrid-prefer-performance-cores",
      "name": "Prefer performance cores for all threads on hybrid CPUs",
      "category": "Performance",
      "impact": "experimental",
      "summary": "Sets the heterogeneous thread scheduling policy to 'Prefer performant processors'.",
      "description": "On CPUs with performance and efficiency cores, Windows 11 chooses a core class per thread from its Quality of Service level. Two hidden power settings override the preference for long-running threads (SCHEDPOLICY) and short-running threads (SHORTSCHEDPOLICY): 0 all, 1 performant only, 2 prefer performant, 3 efficient only, 4 prefer efficient, 5 automatic (Windows decides from the system and QoS). This sets both to 2 on AC power in the active plan.",
      "risk": "systemConfiguration",
      "level": "moderate",
      "benefit": "unverified",
      "valueBasis": "documented",
      "activation": "immediate",
      "minBuild": 22000,
      "limitations": "Only meaningful on hybrid CPUs (for example Intel 12th gen and later, some Arm parts); elsewhere the setting exists but changes nothing. Microsoft does not say that Automatic is the default and documents no benefit. The likely effect is that low-priority and background threads are also pushed onto performance cores, where they can compete with a game for the same cores and draw more power. It is a hidden setting; AC value only, battery behaviour is unchanged. Mesura offers it only on a PC whose processor reports more than one core class. The benefit is unmeasured.",
      "source": "Microsoft Learn SchedulingPolicy (https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/customize/power-settings/configuration-for-hetero-power-scheduling-schedulingpolicy) and ShortSchedulingPolicy (https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/customize/power-settings/configuration-for-hetero-power-scheduling-shortschedulingpolicy): aliases SCHEDPOLICY and SHORTSCHEDPOLICY, hidden, values 0-5 as listed, Automatic lets the OS decide; Windows 11 supported on x64 and Arm. GUIDs read with powercfg /qh on Windows 11 build 26200: SUB_PROCESSOR 54533251-82be-4824-96c1-47b60b740d00, SCHEDPOLICY 93b8b6dc-0698-4d1c-9ee4-0644e900c85d, SHORTSCHEDPOLICY bae08b81-2d5e-4688-ad6a-13243356654b.",
      "sources": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/customize/power-settings/configuration-for-hetero-power-scheduling-schedulingpolicy",
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/customize/power-settings/configuration-for-hetero-power-scheduling-shortschedulingpolicy",
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/customize/power-settings/configure-processor-power-management-options"
      ],
      "powerSettings": [
        {
          "subgroup": "54533251-82be-4824-96c1-47b60b740d00",
          "setting": "93b8b6dc-0698-4d1c-9ee4-0644e900c85d",
          "name": "Heterogeneous thread scheduling policy",
          "ac": 2
        },
        {
          "subgroup": "54533251-82be-4824-96c1-47b60b740d00",
          "setting": "bae08b81-2d5e-4688-ad6a-13243356654b",
          "name": "Heterogeneous short running thread scheduling policy",
          "ac": 2
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "power.sata-link-power-off-ac",
      "name": "Turn off SATA link power saving (on AC)",
      "category": "Power",
      "impact": "experimental",
      "summary": "SATA drives on AHCI keep their link active instead of powering it down on AC.",
      "description": "Sets the AHCI link power management mode to Active (0) on AC power: Microsoft defines this as link power management not being used, so the SATA link isn't put into a low-power state by the host (HIPM) or the device (DIPM). Balanced uses HIPM (1) on AC on the Windows 11 build 26200 test PC; High performance uses 0.",
      "risk": "systemConfiguration",
      "level": "safe",
      "benefit": "unverified",
      "valueBasis": "documented",
      "activation": "immediate",
      "limitations": "Only for SATA drives behind an AHCI controller; NVMe drives are not affected. The saving at stake is small and so is any benefit: the link doesn't have to wake after idle. No measurement of a speed gain is available. Microsoft's page lists three values (0 Active, 1 HIPM, 2 HIPM and DIPM) while Windows' powercfg also shows DIPM and Lowest; only 0 is used. Active plan only, AC only; some SATA SSD/HDD controllers need link power management to reach deep idle, so a laptop may use more idle power.",
      "source": "Microsoft Learn 'Link power management mode - HIPM/DIPM': GUID 0b2d69d7-a2a1-449c-9680-f91c70521c60, hidden, 0 Active ('Link power management is not used'), 1 HIPM, 2 HIPM and DIPM; Windows 7 and later. Plan defaults (Balanced 1, High performance 0, Power saver 2 on AC) read from powercfg /qh and DefaultPowerSchemeValues on a Windows 11 26200 PC.",
      "sources": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/customize/power-settings/disk-settings-link-power-management-mode---hipm-dipm"
      ],
      "powerSettings": [
        {
          "subgroup": "0012ee47-9041-4b5d-9b77-535fba8b1442",
          "setting": "0b2d69d7-a2a1-449c-9680-f91c70521c60",
          "name": "AHCI link power management - HIPM/DIPM",
          "ac": 0
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "requires": [ "ram16gb" ],
      "id": "memory.keep-kernel-in-ram",
      "name": "Disable paging of kernel code and drivers",
      "category": "System",
      "impact": "experimental",
      "summary": "Keeps kernel and driver code in RAM instead of letting Windows page it to disk.",
      "description": "Sets DisablePagingExecutive to 1. By default (0) Windows may write kernel-mode drivers and system code to the page file when they are idle and read them back when needed. With 1 that code stays in physical memory. Microsoft's reference says the benefit is the avoided disk reads and that it applies to machines with a lot of memory. A restart is required.",
      "risk": "systemConfiguration",
      "level": "moderate",
      "benefit": "unverified",
      "valueBasis": "documented",
      "activation": "reboot",
      "limitations": "Pinned code is memory programs and the file cache can no longer use, so on a PC with little RAM this can make paging of your programs worse, not better. Microsoft's own text hedges the gain ('this would depend on the installed applications and their resource utilization') and the references are for Windows Server 2003 and a 2007 blog post; neither measures Windows 10/11, and Mesura has not measured it either. No frame-rate or latency gain is claimed. Windows default is 0 (the value is normally absent). Use only on a desktop with plenty of free RAM.",
      "source": "Microsoft Learn (archived TechNet, Windows Server 2003 registry reference) 'DisablePagingExecutive': REG_DWORD 0|1, default 0; 0 = drivers and system code can be paged to disk as needed, 1 = they must remain in physical memory; restart required; 'improves performance on machines with a lot of memory, because it prevents disk reads'. Microsoft archived blog SYSK 318 (2007): same values and default 0, 'on machines with a lot of memory, this setting can improve performance, but this would depend on the installed applications and their resource utilization'. No Windows 10/11 page or measurement found (searched).",
      "sources": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/previous-versions/windows/it-pro/windows-server-2003/cc757875(v=ws.10)",
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/archive/blogs/irenak/sysk-318-registry-key-that-controls-device-driver-and-system-code-pagination-to-disk"
      ],
      "registry": [
        {
          "hive": "HKLM",
          "path": "SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\Session Manager\\Memory Management",
          "name": "DisablePagingExecutive",
          "type": "dword",
          "value": "1"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "system.ntfs-8dot3-off",
      "name": "Stop creating 8.3 short file names",
      "category": "System",
      "impact": "experimental",
      "summary": "NTFS stops making a second, DOS-style name for new files.",
      "description": "By default NTFS can keep a second 8.3-style name (like PROGRA~1) next to each long file name, and must look those names up when files are created in a folder. Setting NtfsDisable8dot3NameCreation to 1 turns the creation of new short names off for all volumes. Names that already exist are kept.",
      "risk": "systemConfiguration",
      "level": "moderate",
      "benefit": "unverified",
      "valueBasis": "documented",
      "activation": "reboot",
      "limitations": "Windows 10/11 default is 2 (decided per volume; the system volume normally keeps short names). Programs or old installers that look up a short name for a file created after the change can fail. Existing short names are not removed, and Mesura does not strip them: Microsoft warns that removing them can break applications, including uninstalling. Microsoft recommends 1 for SMB file servers; for a desktop PC it gives no figure and Mesura has not measured any gain. Per-volume control (fsutil 8dot3name set D: 1) is not expressible here.",
      "source": "Microsoft Learn 'fsutil behavior': when disable8dot3 is 0 NTFS creates a second entry for every long name and 'must look up' the 8.3 names when creating files; the parameter writes HKLM\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\FileSystem\\NtfsDisable8dot3NameCreation. Microsoft Learn 'fsutil 8dot3name': 0 enables for all volumes, 1 disables for all volumes, 2 per volume, 3 disables for all but the system volume; warning that removing names may break applications. Microsoft Learn FSCT page: default is 2 on Windows Server 2012/R2, system volume enabled and others disabled, recommended 1 for most SMB file servers. Value 2 read on a Windows 11 Pro machine.",
      "sources": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/administration/windows-commands/fsutil-behavior",
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/administration/windows-commands/fsutil-8dot3name",
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/previous-versions/dn567658(v=vs.85)"
      ],
      "registry": [
        {
          "hive": "HKLM",
          "path": "SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\FileSystem",
          "name": "NtfsDisable8dot3NameCreation",
          "type": "dword",
          "value": "1"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "network.interrupt-moderation-off",
      "name": "Turn off network card interrupt moderation",
      "category": "Network",
      "impact": "experimental",
      "summary": "Network card raises an interrupt per packet instead of batching them.",
      "description": "Interrupt moderation makes a network card wait for more packets, or for a timer, before it interrupts the CPU. Microsoft lists switching it off as a tuning step for the lowest possible latency and says it costs CPU time. This sets the standard NDIS keyword *InterruptModeration to 0 on every adapter whose driver exposes that standard keyword in its Advanced properties; Windows' documented default is 1 (enabled).",
      "risk": "systemConfiguration",
      "level": "moderate",
      "benefit": "documented",
      "valueBasis": "documented",
      "activation": "reboot",
      "requires": [
        "noBattery"
      ],
      "limitations": "Costs CPU: in one community test (a gigabit Intel adapter, about 900 Mbps TCP for 30 s) interrupts rose roughly 29 times with moderation off. The gain is on a microsecond scale; no ping or FPS gain is measured or promised. Only adapters whose driver exposes the standard *InterruptModeration property are touched; many drivers use their own vendor keyword and are left alone. Wi-Fi adapters mostly do not expose it (the test PC's does not, so this was not tested on real hardware). The adapter reads the value when it restarts. Do not use on a laptop on battery or a weak CPU.",
      "source": "Microsoft Learn 'Enumeration Keywords': *InterruptModeration 0 = Disabled, 1 = Enabled (Default); 'Interrupt Moderation' (drivers turn it on by default unless the keyword disables it); 'Network Adapter Performance Tuning': 'Disable the Interrupt Moderation setting for network card drivers that require the lowest possible latency ... this configuration can use more CPU time and it represents a tradeoff'. Net class GUID 4d36e972-... from 'System-Defined Device Setup Classes'. Keyword values are REG_SZ (read on PC-000: *WakeOnMagicPacket is a String). Interrupt counts from the community djdallmann GamingPCSetup network research page (single adapter, not Microsoft data).",
      "sources": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/drivers/network/enumeration-keywords",
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/drivers/network/interrupt-moderation",
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/networking/technologies/network-subsystem/net-sub-performance-tuning-nics",
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/drivers/install/system-defined-device-setup-classes-available-to-vendors",
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/drivers/network/standardized-inf-keywords-for-network-devices",
        "https://djdallmann.github.io/GamingPCSetup/CONTENT/RESEARCH/NETWORK"
      ],
      "registryEach": [
        {
          "hive": "HKLM",
          "pathPattern": "SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\Class\\{4d36e972-e325-11ce-bfc1-08002be10318}\\*",
          "name": "*InterruptModeration",
          "type": "string",
          "value": "0",
          "where": {
            "subkeyExists": "Ndi\\params\\*InterruptModeration"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "network.ethernet-eee-off",
      "name": "Turn off Energy-Efficient Ethernet",
      "category": "Network",
      "impact": "experimental",
      "summary": "Stops a wired link entering low-power idle between packets.",
      "description": "Energy-Efficient Ethernet (IEEE 802.3az) lets a wired link drop into a low-power idle state when there is no traffic and wake when a packet arrives. Microsoft defines the standard keyword *EEE (0 = Disabled, 1 = Enabled, default 1). This sets it to 0 on adapters whose driver exposes it, so the link stays fully powered.",
      "risk": "systemConfiguration",
      "level": "safe",
      "benefit": "unverified",
      "valueBasis": "documented",
      "activation": "reboot",
      "requires": [
        "noBattery"
      ],
      "limitations": "Windows documents the keyword and its default, not a latency or FPS benefit; the wake delay of low-power idle is described only in secondary sources (microseconds), so the benefit is unverified. Uses slightly more power on an idle link. Does nothing on Wi-Fi and on wired drivers that use a vendor-specific EEE keyword instead of the standard *EEE (they are left alone). Not tested on real hardware: the test PC has only a Wi-Fi adapter. Needs an adapter restart, so a reboot. Community advice (not Microsoft) often lists turning EEE off when a link drops or flaps with a particular switch; Mesura does not claim it fixes that.",
      "source": "Microsoft Learn 'Standardized INF Keywords for Power Management': '*EEE: a value that describes whether the device should enable IEEE 802.3az energy-efficient ethernet'; table: 0 Disabled, 1 (Default) Enabled. Net class GUID from 'System-Defined Device Setup Classes'. Keyword values are REG_SZ (read on PC-000).",
      "sources": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/drivers/network/standardized-inf-keywords-for-power-management",
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/drivers/install/system-defined-device-setup-classes-available-to-vendors",
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/drivers/network/enumeration-keywords"
      ],
      "registryEach": [
        {
          "hive": "HKLM",
          "pathPattern": "SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\Class\\{4d36e972-e325-11ce-bfc1-08002be10318}\\*",
          "name": "*EEE",
          "type": "string",
          "value": "0",
          "where": {
            "subkeyExists": "Ndi\\params\\*EEE"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "network.nic-selective-suspend-off",
      "name": "Turn off network adapter selective suspend",
      "category": "Power",
      "impact": "experimental",
      "summary": "Stops Windows idling the adapter between traffic bursts (NDIS drivers)",
      "description": "NDIS selective suspend lets Windows put an idle network adapter into a low-power state after an idle time-out (5 seconds by default, 3 seconds with the screen off) and wake it when traffic arrives. The standardized keyword *SelectiveSuspend = 0 tells the miniport driver not to advertise selective suspend at all. This writes the keyword on every adapter whose driver declares it (Ndi\\Params\\*SelectiveSuspend exists).",
      "risk": "systemConfiguration",
      "level": "safe",
      "benefit": "unverified",
      "valueBasis": "documented",
      "activation": "reboot",
      "requires": [
        "noBattery"
      ],
      "limitations": "Microsoft marks the keyword as for traditional NDIS miniport drivers only and deprecated for NetAdapterCx drivers, so adapters on NetAdapterCx drivers either lack it or ignore it. Which adapters declare it depends on the driver; none of the adapters on the test PC (an Intel Wi-Fi 6 AX201 and virtual adapters) do, so there the entry changes nothing. Cost: higher idle power, so a laptop on battery loses runtime (hence the noBattery requirement). The benefit (no wake-up delay after idle) is plausible but not measured here. Default is enabled (1). Adapter restart or reboot needed. This is separate from the USB hub setting 'USB selective suspend' in the power plan.",
      "source": "Microsoft Learn Standardized INF Keywords for NDIS Selective Suspend: *SelectiveSuspend 0 = Disabled, 1 (Default) = Enabled; if 0 the miniport must not advertise any selective suspend capability; *SSIdleTimeout default 5 s, *SSIdleTimeoutScreenOff default 3 s; keywords are for NDIS miniport drivers only and deprecated in NetAdapterCx.",
      "sources": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/drivers/network/standardized-inf-keywords-for-ndis-selective-suspend"
      ],
      "registryEach": [
        {
          "hive": "HKLM",
          "pathPattern": "SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\Class\\{4d36e972-e325-11ce-bfc1-08002be10318}\\*",
          "name": "*SelectiveSuspend",
          "type": "string",
          "value": "0",
          "where": {
            "subkeyExists": "Ndi\\Params\\*SelectiveSuspend"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "network.teredo-off",
      "name": "Turn off Teredo tunnelling",
      "category": "Network",
      "impact": "privacy",
      "summary": "Removes the Teredo IPv6-over-UDP tunnel; breaks Xbox network features on PC.",
      "description": "Teredo wraps IPv6 in IPv4 UDP so a PC behind NAT can reach other IPv6 peers. This sets the 'Set Teredo State' policy to Disabled, which Microsoft documents as 'No Teredo interfaces are present on the host'. Microsoft's IPv6 article lists this policy as the way to turn off the IPv6 transition tunnels.",
      "risk": "systemConfiguration",
      "level": "moderate",
      "benefit": "documented",
      "valueBasis": "documented",
      "activation": "reboot",
      "requires": [
        "proEdition"
      ],
      "limitations": "Microsoft's Xbox support answer says that without a Teredo IP address you cannot use Xbox Live party chat or multiplayer on the PC; some PC games that use Xbox Live networking can lose chat or matchmaking. Do not use it if you play Xbox Live or Game Pass PC multiplayer. Microsoft's IPv6 article says ISATAP and Teredo are disabled by default in Windows, and on this machine Teredo already reports type 'disabled' (netsh interface teredo show state), so on many PCs the setting changes nothing. No speed gain is claimed. Machine policy: ignored by Windows Home. Needs a restart.",
      "source": "Microsoft Learn Policy CSP ADMX_tcpip, Teredo_State: values Default (default state is Client) / Disabled ('No Teredo interfaces are present on the host') / Client / Enterprise Client; value name Teredo_State (string) confirmed in shipped tcpip.admx. Microsoft Q&A (learn.microsoft.com/en-us/answers/a/4553681): without a Teredo IP address you cannot use Xbox Live party chat or multiplayer; 'netsh interface Teredo set state disable' / 'type=default'. Microsoft KB 929852 lists Teredo State = Disabled as the policy way to remove tunnel interfaces.",
      "sources": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/client-management/mdm/policy-csp-admx-tcpip",
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/answers/a/4553681",
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/troubleshoot/windows-server/networking/configure-ipv6-in-windows"
      ],
      "registry": [
        {
          "hive": "HKLM",
          "path": "SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\TCPIP\\v6Transition",
          "name": "Teredo_State",
          "type": "string",
          "value": "Disabled"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "network.wpad-off",
      "name": "Turn off WPAD proxy auto-discovery",
      "category": "Network",
      "impact": "privacy",
      "summary": "Stops WinHTTP looking for a proxy script (wpad.dat) on the local network.",
      "description": "Web Proxy Auto-Discovery (WPAD) lets Windows find a proxy configuration (PAC file) from the local network or DNS. Microsoft documents a WinHTTP registry value that disables WPAD detection for all proxy-detection calls made through the WinHTTP API, for people who prefer to manage endpoints directly. Anyone on the same network who can answer the WPAD lookup could otherwise point WinHTTP traffic at a proxy of their choosing.",
      "risk": "systemConfiguration",
      "level": "moderate",
      "benefit": "documented",
      "valueBasis": "documented",
      "activation": "reboot",
      "minBuild": 17763,
      "limitations": "Microsoft: after you disable WPAD you must configure every proxy manually, so do not use it on a work or school network that hands out proxy settings automatically. It only covers WinHTTP; Microsoft says to also turn off 'Automatically detect settings' in Settings, because browsers and third-party apps use that setting (not changed here, it is a per-user setting stored in a binary blob). 'nslookup wpad' still resolves, because apps can still query DNS directly. Microsoft's own service guidance marks the WinHTTP Web Proxy Auto-Discovery service 'Do not disable', so the service itself is left alone. Applies from Windows 10 1809. No speed gain is claimed.",
      "source": "Microsoft Learn 'How to disable HTTP proxy features': 'Starting in Windows Server 2019 and Windows 10, version 1809, you can disable WPAD by setting a DWORD value ... HKLM\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Internet Settings\\WinHttp\\DisableWpad to 1'; manual proxy configuration needed afterwards; WPAD should also be disabled in the Settings UI; nslookup WPAD still resolves. 'Security guidelines for system services': WinHttpAutoProxySvc 'Do not disable'. The value is absent on this Windows 11 PC.",
      "sources": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/troubleshoot/windows-server/networking/disable-http-proxy-auth-features",
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/security/windows-services/security-guidelines-for-disabling-system-services-in-windows-server"
      ],
      "registry": [
        {
          "hive": "HKLM",
          "path": "SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Internet Settings\\WinHttp",
          "name": "DisableWpad",
          "type": "dword",
          "value": "1"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "telemetry.inventory-collector-off",
      "name": "Turn off the Inventory Collector (policy)",
      "category": "Privacy",
      "impact": "privacy",
      "summary": "Windows stops inventorying apps, files, devices and drivers for Microsoft.",
      "description": "Sets the Group Policy 'Turn off Inventory Collector'. Microsoft says the Inventory Collector inventories applications, files, devices and drivers on the PC and sends the information to Microsoft to help diagnose compatibility problems; enabling the policy stops that and also stops collection of installation data through the Program Compatibility Assistant.",
      "risk": "systemConfiguration",
      "level": "moderate",
      "benefit": "documented",
      "valueBasis": "documented",
      "activation": "reboot",
      "requires": [
        "proEdition"
      ],
      "limitations": "Windows default: policy not configured, so the collector is on. The data is for compatibility diagnosis. Microsoft does not say whether it also feeds the compatibility checks that decide if a feature update is offered to this PC, and no source settles it; Mesura keeps the compatibility-appraiser tasks for the same reason (KB4525208), so treat it as an open question. The InventorySvc service stays as it is. The policy has no effect when CEIP is off, because the collector is then already off (Microsoft). No performance effect is documented. A Group Policy value for Pro, Enterprise, Education and IoT Enterprise; on Home the operation does not apply.",
      "source": "Microsoft Learn Policy CSP ADMX_AppCompat, AppCompatTurnOffProgramInventory: 'Turn off Inventory Collector', key Software\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\AppCompat, value DisableInventory (https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/client-management/mdm/policy-csp-admx-appcompat). AppCompat.admx on Windows 11 build 26200 defines no explicit enabled value for this policy, so the Group Policy default of 1 applies when enabled. DISA STIG entries cite the same key and value 1 as 'disabled collector' (search result, not fetched in full). Service description read locally with Get-CimInstance Win32_Service.",
      "sources": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/client-management/mdm/policy-csp-admx-appcompat"
      ],
      "registry": [
        {
          "hive": "HKLM",
          "path": "Software\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\AppCompat",
          "name": "DisableInventory",
          "type": "dword",
          "value": "1"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
