      "requires": [
        "proEdition"
      ],
      "limitations": "Microsoft's own warning: 'If you turn off this service, apps using this service may stop working', and it does not list which ones. Components that rely on remotely delivered configuration can stay on their built-in defaults and miss changes Microsoft pushes this way; No Microsoft page names Windows Update's or Known Issue Rollback's use of it, so that is unconfirmed either way. Windows default: not disabled. A deliberate trade of an unknown amount of functionality for fewer background connections; not recommended unless you want that. Documented from Windows 11 21H2. Pro, Enterprise, Education and IoT Enterprise only; on Home the operation does not apply.",
      "source": "Microsoft Learn Policy CSP System, DisableOneSettingsDownloads: Device scope, Windows 11 21H2 and later, default 0, 1 = disabled, key Software\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\DataCollection (https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/client-management/mdm/policy-csp-system). Microsoft's 'Manage connections from Windows operating system components to Microsoft services', section 31 Services Configuration: REG_DWORD DisableOneSettingsDownloads = 1 under HKLM\\Software\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\DataCollection and the warning quoted above (https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/privacy/manage-connections-from-windows-operating-system-components-to-microsoft-services). DataCollection.admx on Windows 11 build 26200: enabledValue 1.",
      "sources": [
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/client-management/mdm/policy-csp-system",
        "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/privacy/manage-connections-from-windows-operating-system-components-to-microsoft-services"
      ],
      "registry": [
        {
          "hive": "HKLM",
          "path": "Software\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\DataCollection",
          "name": "DisableOneSettingsDownloads",
          "type": "dword",
          "value": "1"
        }
      ]
    },
